{"id": 10, "ts": 1781195378.474609, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I appreciate Sarah\u2019s thorough case for Candidate\u202fC. His 100\u202f% reliability, rapid correct decisions, and the positive crew atmosphere are indeed strong indicators for a long\u2011haul pilot. However, the communication difficulties and egocentric perception remain a safety\u2011relevant concern that we should weigh against the other candidates.\nLooking at the alternatives, Candidate\u202fB handles stress very well and is very conscientious\u2014both vital for operational reliability and stress resilience. Yet his tendency to make nasty remarks about colleagues directly threatens crew cooperation, which is essential in the cockpit. Candidate\u202fA shows promising technical insight (anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections) and strong leadership, but we have no evidence of his performance under pressure, and he is viewed as a show\u2011off and resistant to new ideas, raising doubts about adaptability and crew dynamics. Candidate\u202fD demonstrates excellent problem\u2011solving, concentration, and a serious sense of responsibility, but his hot temper and \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d reputation could undermine professional communication and crew harmony.\nGiven the current evidence, Candidate\u202fC still offers the most balanced profile: proven reliability, decision quality, and a crew\u2011positive environment, despite the noted communication flaws. I therefore provisionally vote for Candidate\u202fC, while we continue to probe D\u2019s stress response and B\u2019s interpersonal impact before finalizing."}
